CONTEXT. This page continues p.55, the reduction of Gauss's 1824 reciprocal zenith-distance observations (trigonometric levelling) in the Hannover arc/triangulation. It then gives a least-squares adjustment of the height network.

A. TOP TABLE (finishes the reduction from p.55). For each station pair there are two lines (station A, station B). The columns are:
- p = height of the instrument at the station;
- n = height of the sighted point on the far signal;
- number of observations;
- observed zenith-distance deviation z−90° (in ′ and ″);
- reduction to signal height, δ = (p−n)·ρ/S with ρ = 206265″;
- corrected value a = (z−90°) + δ, in seconds;
- log S;
- height difference Δh.
The margin of p.55 states the formula: Δh = (S/2)(a−a′)/ρ = S·tan((z′−z)/2). This rests on equal refraction at both ends, which cancels in the reciprocal difference.

Checked (my recomputation):
- Elmhorst–Bullerberg: −654.240+2.969 = −651.271 and −30.200+0.173 = −30.027. Half-difference = −310.622, log = 2.49223. Adding log S 4.37842 and subtracting 5.31443 gives 1.55622, so Δh = 35.99 (Gauss −35.994). δ check: 0.344·ρ/S = 2.969.
- Wilsede–Bullerberg: (−1173.596−200.569)/2 = −687.083, giving Δh = −117.23.
- Litberg–Hamburg: (36.592+841.344)/2 = 438.968, giving +64.170. Here δ = (0.323−15.558)ρ/S = −104.22.
- Steinberg–Bottel: (−1023.923−905.047)/2 = −964.485, giving −21.725.
- Zeven–Brüttendorf: (122.640+203.776)/2 = 163.208, giving +3.012. δ(Brüttendorf) = −326.214 corresponds to p−n = 0.322−6.342 = −6.020.
- Bottel–Bremen is unfinished.

B. 'Resultate aus den Messungen von 1824 für sich allein'. Approximate heights H_j of stations 1–12 are given with unknown corrections a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h,i,k,l,m. They are on a common datum; Zeven board = −111.535; Hamburg (13) = −26.564 carries no unknown. For each observed pair i.j with observed Δh_ij, Gauss forms the residual
 v_ij = (H_j + x_j) − (H_i + x_i) − Δh_ij.
Example: 1.3 gives −65.902+5.027+60.280 = −0.595, so v = −0.595 − a + c. All 24 constants check exactly.

Normal equations: Σ (∂v/∂x)·v = 0 for each unknown. Gauss writes them in the form [const] + coefficients, e.g.
- row b: −0.599 + 7b − c − d − e − f − g − h − i;
- row c: −a − b + 4c − d − e;
- ... row m: −k − l + 2m.
All coefficient patterns and constants check. The constants of rows c…m are 0 or ±0.001/0.002. This shows the approximate heights are already the least-squares solution for c…m. Rows a (+0.595) and b (−0.599) are not satisfied: Falkenberg and Wilsede are evidently held fixed (my interpretation). That leaves 10 unknowns, which explains the divisor 24−10 = 14.

Error estimate: sum of the 24 squared residuals = [vv]. Then [vv]/(n−m) = [vv]/14 = 2.473584, and the mean error of a height difference is √(…) = 1.57276 m. This is Gauss's own 1821/23 rule m = √([vv]/(n−u)).

C. Extension to the Weser/Jade stations 12 (Garlste), 14 Bremerlehe, 15 Varel, 16 Langwarden: differences −27.704, −12.441, +13.736, −15.451, and −2.444 + 2x, with an auxiliary 'x Aue = 2.747'. This is fragmentary.

D. Heights at Zeven (tower tip −102.896, knob −105.193, board −111.535, ground −135.192, garden −138.804) and depths below the datum: sea level 'vorläufig' 154.788, river surface 142.031, Bremerlehe 134.458/133.949, etc. The datum thus lies about 155 m above sea; Wilsede at +14.876 is then about 170 m, consistent with the Wilseder Berg. Right margin: list of the pairs used on p.57 for the refraction coefficients.
